From 0d21928e6da125d5e4836405b28687b83a60a089 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: C0fuge <189659128+C0fuge@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 15:19:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] hadoopfs: process-wide metadata cache
 (hdfs_metadata_cache_ttl_ms), lazy open, correct mtime

DuckDB opens a Parquet file several times per query (bind, footer, one
handle per scan state) and every open cost two NameNode calls
(getfileinfo + getBlockLocations); FileExists one more. On a Kerberos/SASL
cluster that is tens of round trips per point query.

- setting hdfs_metadata_cache_ttl_ms (BIGINT ms, env
  HDFS_METADATA_CACHE_TTL_MS, default 0 = off): file status and the
  located blocks of finalized files are cached per namenode+path for that
  long, 10000 paths LRU, process-wide; only successful lookups are stored;
  a fresh status with another length/mtime drops the blocks; writes,
  deletes, renames and truncates invalidate
- with the cache on, a read-only regular file is opened lazily (first
  read/seek) from the cached block locations (libhdfs3 patch 0002
  hdfsOpenFileWithBlockLocations): a repeated open costs no NameNode call;
  the locations cover the whole file, so the footer seek needs none either
- GetLastModifiedTime: mLastMod is in seconds and timestamp_t in
  microseconds; upstream returned seconds*1000 (January 1970)
---
